==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|Himself||honorific}}
* {{alt|en|himselfe||obsolete}}
* {{alt|en|himselve||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|hymself}}, from {{inh|en|ang|[[him]] [[selfum]]}}. Equivalent to {{af|en|him|-self}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/hɪmˈsɛlf/|/ɪ̈mˈsɛlf/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Back ache-himself.wav|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-himself.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/hɪmˈself/|/ɪ̈mˈself/|a=AU}}
* {{hyphenation|en|him|self}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛlf|s=2}}

===Pronoun===
{{en-pron|desc=the third person singular, masculine, personal pronoun|the reflexive form of|he|feminine|herself|neuter|itself|plural|themselves|gender-neutral singular|himself|or|themselves|or|themself}}

# {{lb|en|reflexive pronoun}} [[him|Him]]; {{non-gloss|the male object of a verb or preposition that also appears as the subject}}
#: {{ux|en|He injured '''himself'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=2|passage=Sunning '''himself''' on the board steps, I saw for the first time Mr. Farquhar Fenelon Cooke. He was dressed out in broad gaiters and bright tweeds, like an English tourist, and his face might have belonged to Dagon, idol of the Philistines.}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Hannah Rabinowitz|title=Alleged leaders of White supremacist group charged in effort to encourage terrorism and hate crimes|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2024/09/09/politics/white-supremacist-terrorgram-indictment/index.html|date=2024-09-09|passage=One Terrorgram user livestreamed '''himself''' stabbing five people outside of a mosque in Turkey, she said, and a 19-year-old Slovakian man praised the group in a manifesto before killing two people at an LGBTQ bar in Bratislava, the capital of Slovakia.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260102084301/https://edition.cnn.com/2024/09/09/politics/white-supremacist-terrorgram-indictment/index.html|archivedate=2 January 2026}}
# {{lb|en|emphatic}} {{U|he}}; {{non-gloss|used as an intensifier, often to emphasize that the referent is the exclusive participant in the predicate}}
#: {{ux|en|He was injured '''himself'''.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Isaiah|7|14|page=767|column=2|text=Therefore the Lord '''himſelfe''' ſhal giue you a ſigne:{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2014-06-21|volume=411|issue=8892
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/books-and-arts/21604535-real-sir-isaac-newton-was-not-first-king-reason-last Magician’s brain]
|passage=The [Isaac] Newton that emerges from the [unpublished] manuscripts is far from the popular image of a rational practitioner of cold and pure reason. The architect of modern science was '''himself''' not very modern. He was obsessed with alchemy.}}
# {{lb|en|Ireland|otherwise|_|archaic}} {{n-g|The subject or non-reflexive object of a predicate;}} [[he]] [[himself]].
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=7|book=II|passage=Yet it is that '''himselfe''' had been liberally gratified by his Unkle with militarie rewards, before ever he went to warres.}}
#* Sir {{w|John Denham (poet)|John Denham}} (1614-1669)
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Kirk Jones|title=Waking Ned|publisher=Tomboy films
|passage=''Dennis:'' His glass is there and '''himself''' is in the toilet.}}
# {{lb|en|Ireland}} {{n-g|The subject or non-reflexive object of a predicate;}} [[he]] {{q|used of upper-class gentlemen, or sarcastically, of men who imagine themselves to be more important than others}}
#: {{ux|en|Has '''himself''' come down to breakfast yet?}}
#: {{ux|en|Have you seen '''himself''' yet this morning?}}

