==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|chiden||to chide, rebuke, disapprove, criticize; complain, grumble, dispute; argue, debate, dispute, quarrel}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ċīdan||to chide, reprove, rebuke; blame, contend, strive, quarrel, complain}}. Cognate with {{cog|de|kiden||to sound}}; {{cog|goh|kīdal||wedge}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ t͡ʃaɪd/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-chide.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/t͡ʃɛɪd/|a=Northumbria}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪd|s=1}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|~s|chiding|chid,~d,chode|+,~d,chidden}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|somewhat|literary}} To [[admonish]] in [[blame]]; to [[reproach]] [[angrily]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1594|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=w:The Two Gentlemen of Verona|section=act 2, scene 1|url=http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=twogents&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl|passage={{smallcaps|Valentine}}: Well, you’ll still be too forward.<br/>{{smallcaps|Speed}}: And yet I was last '''chidden''' for being too slow.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260216193618/https://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=twogents&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl|archivedate=16 February 2026}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello Q1|act=II|scene=i|page=24|passage=I know too much: / I finde it, I; for when I ha liſt to ſleepe, / [[marry#Interjection|Mary]], before your Ladiſhip I grant, / She puts her tongue alittle in her heart, / And '''chides''' with thinking.|translation=<nowiki>I know, [she talks] too much: / I find that, when I have desire to sleep. / Indeed, before your Ladyship I admit, / She keeps a little quiet, / And '''scolds''' me with her thoughts.</nowiki>}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1795|author=|title={{w|Gammer Gurton's Garland|Gammer Gurton's Garland, or, The Nursery Parnassus}}|location=Stockton|publisher=R. Christopher|page=27|text=Yet didn't you ſee, yet didn't you ſee,<br>What naughty tricks they put upon me:<br>They broke my pitcher,<br>And ſpilt the water,<br>And huff'd my mother,<br>And '''chid''' her daughter,<br>And kiſs'd my ſiſter inſtead of me.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To utter words of [[disapprobation]] and displeasure; to find fault; to contend angrily.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1611|title={{w|King James Version}} of the {{w|Bible}}|section={{w|Book of Genesis|Genesis}} 31:36|url=https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Genesis+31&version=KJV|text=And Jacob was wroth, and '''chode''' with Laban: and Jacob answered and said to Laban, What is my trespass? what is my sin, that thou hast so hotly pursued after me?|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261003065924/https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Genesis+31&version=KJV|archivedate=3 October 2026}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|obsolete}} To make a [[clamorous]] [[noise]]; to [[chafe]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1612|author=w:William Shakespeare; {{w|John Fletcher (playwright)|John Fletcher}}|title={{w|Henry VIII (play)|Henry VIII}}|section=act 3, scene 2|url=http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=henry8&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl|passage={{...}} though perils did<br/>Abound, as thick as thought could make ’em, and<br/>Appear in forms more horrid,—yet my duty,<br/>As doth a rock against the '''chiding''' flood,<br/>Should the approach of this wild river break,<br/>And stand unshaken yours.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250706163627/https://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=henry8&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl|archivedate=6 July 2025}}

